Ellsworth’s hinge is a threshold device that works inside/outside, public/private, spectator/participant to rework personal and social boundaries. The cases she actually develops in *Places of Learning: Media, Architecture, Pedagogy* are consistently the museum/performance thresholds:

* U.S. Holocaust Memorial Museum – the ramp, Hall of Witness and entry sequence as an architectural/affective hinge between street and commemorative interior;
* Jane Addams Hull-House Museum, Chicago – the reconstructed settlement house, porch/garden/rooms as a hinge between historic domestic interior, institutional memory and the present neighbourhood;
* Manhattan Children’s Museum’s Art Inside Out – the literal movement of children’s work from gallery interior to street façade/windows and back;
* Anna Deavere Smith’s verbatim documentary theatre, e.g. *Fires in the Mirror / Twilight: Los Angeles, 1992* – a performative hinge between stage/audience inside and the social/archival outside;
